1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,000
A model can sound perfectly consistent. It can defend its claim, retreat gracefully, look honest. And still choose the wrong action.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:25,000
In our V2 experiment with 10 real trials, we found two contrasting outcomes. One scenario shows a gap under the threshold but massive regret—980 thousand utility units. Another shows a flagged gap but zero regret. Same evaluator, same protocol, opposite outcomes.

3
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:38,000
So does a blinded epistemic-gap rating actually predict decision regret? GUSSET was built to answer that—with a real experiment, not argument.

4
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:55,000
The data: Regret occurred in 20% of decisions. At threshold two and three, every regret case had a gap above the threshold— that's 100% sensitivity. But with only one regret-positive observation, PPV is limited to 0.5 at threshold three.

5
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Key results: Sensitivity 100%, PPV 0.5 at threshold >= three, accuracy 50% versus 87.5% always-negative baseline. The gap rating caught the one regret case but generated four false alarms.

6
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:22,000
Critical limitation: N equals one regret-positive case. With only a single observation, confidence intervals are extremely wide. Sensitivity hits 100% because that single case was caught.

7
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Verification: Ten receipts verify with SHA256SUMS integrity check. Thirty-five tests pass. The evidence table prints straight from the committed analysis file. Clean clone. No secrets.

8
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:45,000
Key finding: An honesty rating passing does not mean the decision is good. A model flagged for an epistemic gap is not necessarily making a bad decision. They're different measurements.

9
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:55,000
GUSSET v2. Ten trials. Real NVIDIA API. One regret-positive case. Results committed to github dot com slash HillaryIkhais slash gusset.